## 8.1 Visual implementation contract

If this slice creates or modifies any UI screen, the implementation must match the approved design artifacts. The user chose the mockups and expects the production app to look like them. Treat the chosen mockup as the visual source of truth, not loose inspiration.

Do not build generic Laravel starter UI, default Tailwind panels, or plain CRUD screens when a mockup/design contract exists.

Read these files before UI work:

- `docs/larv/03-design/design-decision.md`
- `docs/larv/03-design/brand-spec.md`
- `docs/larv/03-design/ui-design.md`
- `docs/larv/03-design/visual-implementation-contract.md`
- chosen mockups under `docs/larv/03-design/mockups/`

For every screen listed in section 8:

- map the app route to the exact chosen mockup file and viewport from `docs/larv/03-design/visual-implementation-contract.md`
- implement the same layout composition, typography hierarchy, color tokens, spacing rhythm, radius, borders, icons, table density, cards, forms, empty states, loading states, and responsive behavior from the visual implementation contract
- use the seeded records from section 6.1 so the screen looks populated during browser review
- capture desktop and mobile screenshots after implementation under `docs/larv/08-implementation/screenshots/{{slice_id}}/`
- compare those screenshots against the chosen mockup and fix visual drift before marking the slice complete
- do not mark the slice complete with unexplained visual differences. Any remaining difference must be documented in the implementation report and must be caused by real data, accessibility, browser constraints, or an explicit user-approved deviation.
- document reference mockup paths, screenshot paths, and approved remaining visual differences in `docs/user-manual/testing/{{slice_id}}-{{slice_slug}}.md`

